    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Canon is the standard scripture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, kept in Pāli and arranged in three baskets: Vinaya (monastic discipline), Sutta (discourses) and Abhidhamma (systematic doctrine).</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> tradition traces it to the Buddha and his early disciples, while scholars distinguish earlier and later strata and disagree about how much goes back to the Buddha himself.</li><li>It was recited orally for centuries before it was written down in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and memorisation and recitation remain part of Buddhist practice.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A Burmese monk named Vicittasara learned the entire Canon by heart for the Sixth Council, according to the usual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> numbering.</p></aside>
    </section>

